From 769a02a71db684e20280cb7a64d78c329e9b8ffb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luca Bruni <luca.bruni@amd.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 16:19:44 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] EXPERIMENTAL clr-pm4-host-stream-wait

Opt-in prototype, not for upstream as is.

hipStreamWaitValue32/64 defaults to a GPU spin kernel. On PM4-emulated queues
(ROCr HSA_AMD_AGENT_INFO_PM4_EMULATION) under a KMD without compute
preemption or hardware scheduling (legacy gfx9 Windows KMD), the spin holds the
engine while the write it waits for sits queued behind it on another queue,
until the watchdog fires.

For host memory on PM4-emulated queues, submit a barrier that depends on a
signal (the PM4 emulation resolves it on the CPU) and have a CPU thread poll
the value and release the signal. Device memory keeps the GPU spin kernel.
GPU_STREAMOPS_HOST_WAIT=0 disables it.

Validated at the 7.15 nightly pin on gfx90c: Unit_hipStreamWaitValue_Default
uint32/uint64 FAIL -> PASS, the other 21 stream-value tests unchanged,
hip-tests 2587/23/335 vs 2585/25/335, zero regressions. Rebased onto the
current pin; flags.hpp context changed (HIPRTC_USE_RUNTIME_UNBUNDLER removed
upstream), no logic change.
